  "_close_classes": "A row with no `code` is a close that implies no relay code. The two languages spell that absence differently on purpose: Go carries CloseInfo.Code as 0 and TypeScript leaves CloseInfo.code undefined, which is the idiom each language reads as empty. The vector drives both from the same absent member, so neither spelling can drift into meaning something else: Go unmarshals a missing number as 0 and TypeScript reads a missing member as undefined, and each test asserts against exactly that. Fifteen of the rows below exercise it. 0 is not a relay code and never will be, which is what makes the Go spelling safe.",
  "close_classes": [
    {"name": "normal closure", "close_code": 1000, "class": "clean"},
    {"name": "going away", "close_code": 1001, "class": "clean"},
    {"name": "protocol error, raised by the library", "close_code": 1002, "class": "invalid_frame", "code": 477},
    {"name": "unsupported data", "close_code": 1003, "class": "invalid_frame", "code": 477},
    {"name": "message too big, raised by the library", "close_code": 1009, "class": "invalid_frame", "code": 477},
    {"name": "abnormal closure, synthesized locally", "close_code": 1006, "class": "transport"},
    {"name": "internal error", "close_code": 1011, "class": "transport"},
    {"name": "no status received", "close_code": 1005, "class": "unknown"},
    {"name": "invalid frame payload data", "close_code": 1007, "class": "unknown"},
